schema: test/v2
level: working-unit
epic: E-EXPORT
wu: E-EXPORT.W2
branch: wu/export/har-out
tests:
  unit:
    count: 28
    covers: "src/api/har.rs (24 + 1 proptest) - the document envelope (version 1.2, creator name and crate version, entry count) and that entries keep the order they were given; the full request/response field mapping for a POST with bodies, cookies and a query; a flow still awaiting its response omitted, both alone and mixed with a complete one; an unmeasured duration reported as HAR's -1 in both time and timings.wait rather than a zero; header lists preserving order and duplicates; headersSize -1 with body sizes reported where measured; query-string parsing across five shapes (absent, single, repeated key, percent-encoded, empty value); cookies from a Cookie header and from multiple Set-Cookie headers, and empty lists where neither is present; redirectURL from Location and empty without it; statusText canonical for known codes and empty for an unknown one; all six body cases (UTF-8 text as text with no encoding field; binary as base64 under HAR's encoding; a text-typed body whose bytes are not UTF-8 falling back to base64 rather than lossily replacing them; Empty as a measured zero with no reason; Streaming and ReadError as size -1 with no text plus a comment naming the reason; TooLarge reporting the size it had without the bytes); postData carrying a captured request body in both text and base64 forms and omitted for all three bodyless states; the octet-stream fallback when no content type was captured; the reason-token helper answering only for the three states that lost bytes; and an_unmeasured_duration_never_puts_a_negative_number_in_timings, the named regression test for the review's one confirmed correctness finding - HAR sanctions -1 for entry.time but requires timings' three phases to be non-negative, and an unmeasured duration propagated the sentinel into wait. That state is unreachable from the capture path (store.rs assigns response and duration_ms atomically under one lock from recorder.rs's single call site) but Flow::duration_ms is a public field, so W3's importer reading an entry with time: -1 would construct it and export invalid HAR; the test was proved to fail without the fix. Proptest every_entry_carries_the_har_1_2_required_fields is the WU's central claim - over generated flows (three methods, four URI shapes including an unparseable one, all five body variants on both legs, present and absent responses, present and absent durations) every produced entry carries every HAR-required key on the entry, the request and the response, timings sums to time whenever time is known, and no body object carries text without a truthful size nor omits text without either a reason or a measured zero. Strengthened by the review to assert each timing phase is non-negative rather than merely present - checking presence alone is what let the negative wait through. A regression seed captured from a genuine failure during the work is committed at proptest-regressions/api/har.txt. src/api/routes.rs (3) - the export served with the JSON content type and an attachment Content-Disposition naming a trawler-<stamp>.har file, an empty capture yielding a valid document with no entries, and the filter reaching the store. src/api/mod.rs (1) - export_is_a_route_of_its_own_not_a_flow_id asserts axum prefers the static /flows/export segment over /flows/{id}, because that precedence would regress silently."
  integration:
    count: 5
    covers: "tests/api.rs over the real axum router. export_serves_a_har_1_2_document_of_the_capture asserts every field the requirement names on a seeded flow - log.version, log.creator.name, startedDateTime present, a non-negative time equal to timings.wait, method, url, both httpVersions, the parsed queryString and cookies, postData text, status, canonical statusText, content text and size, and the required cache object. export_and_listing_select_the_same_flows runs six filter shapes (none, host, method, status, content_type, q) against both GET /flows and GET /flows/export and compares the URL sequences - the epic's expected result 3, which additionally holds by construction because both handlers deserialize one FilterQuery and convert it the one way. export_of_an_empty_capture_is_a_valid_empty_har covers both an empty store and a filter matching nothing. export_marks_uncaptured_bodies_rather_than_reporting_them_empty is the honest-body contract as a client sees it - a streamed body at size -1 with no text and a streaming reason, a too-large body reporting 9000000 with no text and a too_large reason, and a genuinely empty body as a measured zero carrying no reason at all. the_har_document_shape_is_locked is the insta snapshot over a four-flow fixture (text response, binary request body, streamed response, too-large body) that discharges the WU obligation to snapshot-lock a new wire contract, with the two nondeterministic fields (startedDateTime, the creator's crate version) normalised first per the testing contract's ban on asserting exact timestamps."
  e2e:
    waived: "The export's e2e is the epic's own tests/e2e_export.rs step 3 (GET /flows/export parsed as HAR 1.2, asserting entries carry method, URI, status, versions, headers, timings and bodies and that a skipped body is not reported as empty), which lands with the epic per its e2e plan. A second e2e here would boot a second engine to drive the same seam through the same transport. The regression evidence that this WU left the capture path it only reads untouched is that both existing e2e suites (e2e_capture, e2e_h2) run green unchanged and no pre-existing assertion was altered. The seam was additionally hand-run against the live binary: real proxied traffic through :7420, then GET /flows/export on :7421 returned application/json with a trawler-20260904-091931.har attachment carrying one entry, log.version 1.2, statusText OK, time 170 with timings.wait 170 and the captured HTML body as readable text; ?host=example exported exactly what /flows?host=example listed and ?host=nope exported an empty entry list."
note: "E-EXPORT.W2 delivers GET /flows/export as a HAR 1.2 document of the filtered capture, over a new src/api/har.rs that projects Flow onto a HAR entry. Two things carry the WU. First, the export and the listing cannot select different sets because the filter has one definition: the filter fields moved out of ListQuery into FilterQuery plus one From impl, with paging's two parameters split into PagingQuery, and both endpoints deserialize the former. Second, every fact Trawler does not hold is HAR's own sentinel rather than an invented value - headersSize -1, redirectURL empty, cache empty, time -1 when unmeasured - and the three body states HAR cannot express (streamed, too large, read error) carry no text plus a comment naming the reason, so a 4MB event stream can never be mistaken for a 204. A too-large body reports the size it had, because that size is known and is exactly why the bytes were dropped. Bodies are not decoded: the exported bytes are the captured bytes with the Content-Encoding that describes them still attached, so the file never contradicts its own headers. Two mechanism choices differ from what the plan assumed, both recorded in the result: the shared filter is two Query extractions rather than a #[serde(flatten)] member, because serde_urlencoded (which axum's Query uses) cannot deserialize a flattened struct from a query string - the first attempt turned every unparameterised GET /flows into a query rejection, caught immediately by the pre-existing list_flows_orders_and_filters, and the constraint is now recorded in FilterQuery's doc comment; and an uncaptured request body omits postData entirely (with bodySize -1 carrying the fact) because HAR's postData has no size field and its text is required, so an empty text would claim the request had no body. redirectURL needed an explicit serde rename because HAR spells it with a capital URL, which camelCase alone cannot produce - found by the field-mapping unit test. insta joins as a dev-dependency per the epic plan's question 7, and form_urlencoded as a dependency (already in the tree via axum's own serde_urlencoded) for the queryString parsing. No UI-rendering file in the diff. cargo test green (108 lib, 20 api, 3 ca, 6 e2e_capture, 4 e2e_h2); clippy pedantic clean with --all-targets --all-features; fmt clean. The code review confirmed three findings, all nits, all fixed rather than waived: the negative timing phase above; rfc3339 defined byte-identically in har.rs and view.rs, so the wire timestamp format now has one definition as api::rfc3339 shared by both projections; and a code comment claiming the reason tokens came from BodyRec::state, which structurally cannot produce too_large or read_error - it now names BodyView's reason, which is what DEC-30 already recorded. Six candidates were refuted, including a literal + in a query decoding as a space (the wire-exact URI is preserved in request.url and DEC-30 excludes queryString from round-trip fidelity), the proposal to move the token mapping into the domain model (which would push wire vocabulary below the API boundary), and the export's CPU cost (the same execution-context pattern list_flows and get_flow already use, measured at the shipped 256MB default cap). A second review round over the fixed tip found five more nits, four of them one real miss: clamping the timing phases introduced an exception to an invariant three places still asserted (HarTimings' doc comment, UC-6's sentinel sentence, DEC-30's round-trip row all claimed the block sums to the total, which cannot hold when the total is -1 and the phases may not be negative) - each now states the exception and why HAR forces it. The fifth: the replacement token comment attributed all three names to BodyView::NotCaptured's reason field when streaming is that enum's variant tag, so it now claims only that the two surfaces agree. rfc3339 also moved from api/mod.rs, which otherwise holds only router composition and the CORS layer, into view.rs beside the projections that use it. Round 2 independently re-verified all three round-1 fixes, including that the new regression test fails without its fix. A third round over the fixed tip returned no findings. Two nits were accepted as waivers on the cr/v3 note. The result report also records one deviation the rounds surfaced: the plan's mapping table specifies timings as wait: time unconditionally, which the code cannot honour because HAR forbids a negative phase - the plan stands as the record of intent and the result names the corrected row as the one W3 reads when it inverts the projection. The review's measurement also corrected the result report's own claim that the ring cap bounds export memory: peak is roughly 4x the cap, and the follow-up row now says so."
